.TH PHYS 2
.SH NAME
phys \- allow a process to access physical addresses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP
PDP-11:
.B "phys(segreg, size, physadr)"
.TP
8086:
.B "phys(0, 0, physadr)"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B phys
allows a process to access physical memory, normally not in the
process' address space.
This call is obviously machine dependent and
very dangerous.
Its arguments and actions differ somewhat between
PDP-11 and 8086 processors:
.TP
PDP-11:
The argument
.I segreg
specifies a process virtual (data-space)
address range of 8K bytes starting at virtual address
.IR segreg \(mu8K
bytes.
This address range is mapped into physical address
.IR physadr \(mu64
bytes.
Only the first
.IR size \(mu64
bytes of this mapping is
addressable.
If
.I size
is zero, any previous mapping of this virtual
address range is nullified.
For example, the call
.sp
.ti +5
phys(6, 1, 0177775);
.sp
will map virtual addresses 0140000\-0140077 into physical
addresses 017777500\-017777577.
In particular, virtual address
0140060 is the PDP-11 console located at physical address
017777560.
.TP
8086:
The user's extra segment is mapped into physical address
.IR physadr \(mu512
bytes.
If
.I physadr
is \-1, any previous mapping of this
virtual address range is nullified.
For example, the call
.sp
.ti +5
phys(0, 0, 0XB800/(512/16));
.sp
will map extra segment addresses 0 to 0XFFFF into physical
addresses B8000\-C7FFF.
.sp
After mapping via
.BR phys ,
the extra segment may be read and written
with the calls described in
.BR getes (3).
.PP
This call may only be executed by the super-user or if the caller's group
ID is zero.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
getes(3), sdata(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The function value zero is returned if the physical mapping is in effect.
The value \-1 is returned if not super-user, or super group, or an
.B sdata
call is in effect.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(phys = 52.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys phys; segreg; size; physadr
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 52
AX = ?
DX = ?
CX = physadr
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
